<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ausiàs March</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1400–<a href="/years/1459">1459</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ausiàs March was a Valencian knight and poet, the leading lyric poet of medieval Catalan literature. Breaking with the Occitan troubadour tradition that earlier Catalan poets had followed, he wrote introspective verse on love, death and faith in his own language. His work, written during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>'s fifteenth-century golden age, was widely read in the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and influenced Castilian poets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ausias-march.jpg" alt="Ausiàs March" width="320" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacomart · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ausias_Marc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Not much is known of Ausiàs March's life. He was born in about 1400 in Gandia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, to a noble family. His father, Pere March, was himself a poet and served at the court of Peter, the younger brother of King Alfonso IV, and his uncle Jaume March II was also a poet. In 1413 he became head of the family, part of the Valencian petty nobility, on his father's death. From a young age he took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> expeditions of King Alfons el Magnànim. After returning in 1427 he settled in Gandia and never again left the region where he was born. He married twice, first Isabel Martorell, sister of the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanot-martorell">Joanot Martorell</a>, and then Joana Escorna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He inherited an easy fortune from his father, who was treasurer to the Duke of Gandia, and enjoyed the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-viana">Charles of Viana</a>, prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, so he could devote himself to poetry. He was an avowed follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and addressed his Cants d'amor, love songs, to a lady he professed to have first seen in church on Good Friday. He reproduced Petrarch's rhythms as far as the difference of language allowed, yet he is regarded as a very original and idiosyncratic poet who developed Petrarch's rhetoric for inner psychological meditation. In the Cants de mort, the death hymns, he strikes a note of brooding sentiment peculiar to himself.</p><p>He was one of the first poets to use the local vernacular, Valencian, instead of the troubadour language, Occitan. His poems are marked by obscurity, a sometimes monotonous morbidity and a conflict between desire and morality, which reaches its height in the great Cant Spiritual. His verses circulated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> until the first printed edition in Valencian in 1543, but a Spanish translation of 1539 had already made them known. The success of his innovation no doubt encouraged Boscán to introduce Italian metres into Castilian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1450 March moved from Gandia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, where he died on 3 March 1459. He was buried in his family's chapel in Valencia Cathedral, while his two wives and family are buried in the Monastery of Sant Jeroni de Cotalba. Five illegitimate children, but no legitimate heirs, are attributed to him. Raimon, Joan Brudieu and other composers have set his poetry to music.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ausiàs March” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…literature and learning were rich: James I left the Llibre dels fets, the chronicles of Bernat Desclot, Ramon Muntaner and Peter IV followed, Ramon Llull wrote widely in Catalan, Arabic and Latin, and Valencia produced the poet <b>Ausiàs March</b> and the novel Tirant lo Blanc, printed in 1490.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…a major literary language: Ramon Llull wrote philosophy and fiction in it, the chronicles of James I, Bernat Desclot, Ramon Muntaner and Peter IV are major works of medieval historiography, and the wider Catalan-speaking world produced the poetry of <b>Ausiàs March</b> and the chivalric novel Tirant lo Blanc.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 15th century Valencia was the Crown's largest city, building the Silk Exchange and producing writers such as <b>Ausiàs March</b> and Joanot Martorell.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-valencia">Kingdom of Valencia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Beniarjó, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.93,-0.19&amp;year=1438" aria-label="Beniarjó on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4545 767 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ausiàs March"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4995 1037h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4995 1037h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.93,-0.19&amp;year=1438">What was here in 1438? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
